 is Tracking (people) 25+10, Wilderness Survival 30+10. Languages Faerie, traditional Japanese and Dragonese at 98%: Japanese as Language: Native Tongue, Dragonese as Language: Dragonese, Faerie as a Language: Other pick. Literate in traditional Japanese (Literacy: Native Language 40+20) and Dragonese/Elf (Literacy: Dragonese/Elven 30+20). || RELATED: the book prints R.C.C. Related Skills: four, plus one at levels 3, 6, 9 and 12; secondary two at levels 1, 3, 5 and 10, from the same list without the bonuses. Technical ''except computers or any tech skills'' excludes Computer Operation, Computer Programming, Cyberjacking, Cybernetics: Basic, General Repair & Maintenance, Jury-Rig, Recognize Machine Quality and Photography (a judgement: the book names no rows). W.P. ''ancient only'' excludes every firearm, energy, heavy and launcher W.P. the catalog holds. || HAND TO HAND: Teng-jutsu, whose level table lives on the catalog skill row; ''player characters only know Teng-jutsu'' is hand_to_hand costs {}. The style''s mystic powers (printed 69-70) are special abilities of this race: Tengu Acrobatics'' +3 roll with punch is in the class bonuses (every tengu has it); break fall, balance, climb rope, tightrope, leap dodge and timed leap figures have no sheet key and are prose. || DISCREPANCY: the Power Kick is 5D6 M.D. for a tengu in the Teng-jutsu text (printed 70) but 4D6 M.D. in the race''s Damage table (printed 68); both are recorded, the Teng-jutsu figure in the ability. || EQUIPMENT: robe or tunic as robe, sleeping roll as sleeping-bag, water skin as water-skin, the single sword as a choice of katana-rifts or wakizashi-rifts, and ''and/or club or staff'' as a second choice of catalog clubs and staves."
---

## Lore

Tengu are the winged mountain goblins of Japan: short, muscular bird-people with golden or amber eyes, bushy brows, and either a bird-like beak or a huge pointed nose. Their skin is pale pink, white or blue and their wing feathers dark blue, black or dark red. They live in the forested mountains of Japan, Korea, China and India, and are unknown in Europe and the Americas.

They are famous tricksters. Their favorite targets are Buddhist priests and anyone who "worships technology" - especially so-called samurai, ninja and priests who lean on power armor, bionics or Juicer conversions. A tengu will stir such people into brawls and feuds, then step back to enjoy the chaos. Lesser pranks include moving (never stealing) valuables, lifting people onto treetops, stones through windows at night, tied shoelaces and hot-foots. The gift for starting fights has made some people see them as harbingers of war, and the evil ones truly relish it.

Tengu respect true samurai, mystic ninja, Shinto priests, monks and those close to nature, and fear demon quellers. They are fondest of the yamabushi mountain priests, whom even the worst pranksters listen to; some tengu and yamabushi become lifelong travelling companions. Good tengu protect mortals from their evil kin and from demons, guard Shinto temples, and leave anonymous gifts for priests. Evil tengu extort offerings from villagers, rob, kidnap and torment travelers, burn shrines, and often disguise themselves as humans to do it. Scores of tengu live along Tengu Road on Mt. Kurama.

Every tengu learns Teng-jutsu from an early age, an acrobatic fighting art of kicks, back flips and leaping dodges. They are old enemies of the Chinese Celestial Dogs, the gods and minions of China and India, and most demons, and allies of humble farmers, yamabushi, Shinto priests, dragons, great warriors and philosophers.

## Teng-jutsu

Known at first level: break fall, roll with punch, maintain balance, back flip escape and attack, cartwheel attack, body block/tackle, pull punch, kick attack (2D4), karate kick (2D6), karate punch (2D4), knee and elbow strikes (1D6), strike, parry and dodge, plus the tengu mystic powers. The level-by-level bonuses are on the Hand to Hand: Teng-jutsu skill.

## GM Notes

The book calls the tengu a fun player character and an equally good villain: evil and anarchist tengu are opportunists who run with bandits, assassins, sorcerers and demons for mayhem or gain. Average N.P.C. level is 2D4.
', 'published', 'data-script'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'tengu');


-- Read the result back rather than trusting the exit code. d1-apply prints
-- these, and a CR in the stored markdown means the checkout mangled the file.
SELECT class_id, name, status, length(markdown) AS bytes, instr(markdown, char(13)) > 0 AS has_cr
  FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'tengu';

